  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1258 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nThis Afternoon - Friday: Mild Weather and Dry Conditions\n\nAn H500 center of low pressure is currently located over the \nCentral Plains and should continue to slowly dig southeast \ntowards the northern half of the Gulf Coast states before \nlifting northeast on Thanksgiving into Friday. This system is \nforecast to lead to widespread precipitation for much of the \neastern US with winter weather for portions of the Midwest. That\npotential is expected to stay well to the south of the Upper \nMississippi River valley so dry conditions are forecast through \nFriday. Mid to upper level moisture will remain in place through\nat least Friday morning so mostly cloudy skies are expected \nthrough the Thanksgiving holiday. Thankfully, no travel impacts \nare expected given the anticipated dry conditions with a light \nnorth wind. Temperatures should remain near normal due a cooler \nairmass over the region.\n\nThis Weekend - Light Precipitation Chances\n\nA secondary, quick moving, trough is expected to move east from\nthe Northern Plains to the Upper Midwest late Friday into early\nSaturday morning. This system will provide ample lift to \ngenerate light precipitation, if enough moisture is in place. \nForecast soundings indicate a noticeable lack of low level \nmoisture when lift is most prevalent over the region, thus any \nprecipitation that is able to develop should remain quite light.\nGiven the cooler temperatures throughout the atmospheric \ncolumn, we cannot rule out a wintry mix of precipitation rather \nthan just a light, cold rain. The light precipitation potential \nshould quickly wane beyond sunrise as the lift provided by the \ntrough exits off to the east. For the rest of Saturday, \ndecreasing cloud cover and southwesterly to westerly winds \nshould allow for temperatures to rise up into the mid 40s to \nlower 50s for highs.\n\nAnother trough should dig south over the Plains before quickly \nlifting northeast towards New England on Sunday. This system may\nlead to further chances for precipitation from the Plains to \nthe Midwest on Sunday. Recent forecast trends are not as \nfavorable for widespread precipitation across the Driftless \nRegion, as the system is looking to dig further south than \npreviously anticipated. The latest 25/12z forecast model cluster\nanalysis depicts this trend very well, with 3 of the 4 clusters\nof the LREF indicating very little to no rainfall for the 50th \npercentile of each cluster. In fact, the cluster with the \nhighest rain amounts is the one with the least amount of members\n(only 15% of the total ensemble). This is a stark difference \ncompared to the 25/00z guidance, which had at least widespread \nlight precipitation in over half of the ensemble output. AI \ndriven forecast models are also on this trend (the EC-AIFS \nalways favored a southern solution) so confidence in widespread \nprecipitation on Sunday is decreasing. Current NBM PoPs still \nhave widespread 20-40% chances on Sunday but I would expect \nthose to sharply decrease over the next 24 hours. If any \nprecipitation does occur, it would likely be a cold, light rain \nwith the best chances south of I-90. Temperatures on Sunday \nshould drop back down to near or just below normal behind a cold\nfrontal passage.\n\nNext Week - Seasonal and Dry\n\nBeyond this upcoming weekend, zonal upper level flow is \nexpected to prevail over the region to start the next work week.\nThis pattern is not favorable for precipitation so dry \nconditions are currently anticipated beyond Sunday. After a \nbrief cool down on Sunday due to the trough passing overhead, \ntemperatures should slowly rise back up to above normal during \nthe first half of next week.",
